    ["game_files",
    ".SHA",
    "GodMode9 SHA SysNAND Crosscheck File",
    "4.0",
    "An SHA file is a checksum file created by GodMode9, a Nintendo 3DS file browser. It contains a value used to verify the integrity of a Nintendo 3DS SysNAND backup. SHA files are created using the SHA-256 checksum algorithm. GodMode9 allows users to back up the data stored on their Nintendo 3DSs internal storage system, which is commonly referred to as SysNAND. When a user backs up their data, GodMode9 creates a.BIN file that contains the backed-up data and an SHA file that contains a checksum. Before the user stores their BIN file or uses the file to restore their data, they can use the SHA file to verify the BIN files integrity. For example, a user can: Create an SHA file for their SysNAND contents before backing up their data. Back up their data and produce a second SHA file. Compare the two files values to ensure their data was backed up correctly. You can open an SHA file in any text editor, such as Microsoft Notepad (Windows), Apple TextEdit (Mac), or GitHub Atom (cross-platform). Doing so will allow you to view the checksum value your SHA file contains (and likely, compare it to the value stored in another SHA file). Windows Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Linux Vim. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS.",
    "Windows Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Linux Vim. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".QMOD",
    "Beat Saber for Quest Mod",
    "4.0",
    "A QMOD file is a mod for the Oculus Quest version of Beat Saber, a virtual reality (VR) rhythm game in which players slice blocks in time to music. It contains a custom item or user interface element that can be added to the game. Players install QMOD files using BMBF, a modding application. BMBF is an app that Beat Saber for Quest players can install on the Oculus Quest. Players use BMBF to download custom songs and install Beat Saber mods. These mods are saved as QMOD files. Examples of mods that can be added to Beat Saber for Quest using BMBF include: Custom saber models Enhanced score tracking The ability to search for and download songs The ability to hide note scores. QMOD files are not meant to be manually opened. You can install a QMOD file in Beat Saber for Quest by following the instructions included with BMBF. All Platforms BMBFFree.",
    "All Platforms BMBF.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".SABER",
    "Beat Saber Custom Saber",
    "4.0",
    "A SABER file is a custom saber meant to be imported into Beat Games Beat Saber or its open-source clone, Open Saber VR. Beat Saber modders create and distribute SABER files online, via the BeastSaber website and the Beat Saber Discord server. Beat Saber is a VR-based rhythm game in which players slice blocks with sabers in time to music. If a player is dissatisfied with Beat Sabers default sabers, they can download and install custom sabers, saved as SABER files. Modders create custom sabers in Beat Saber, using a plug-in such as Saber Factory 2, or in Open Saber VR. They then export the sabers as SABER files, so they and other players can import and select the sabers in-game. NOTE: The Oculus Quest version of Beat Saber uses custom sabers saved as.ZIP or QSABER files. Only the PC version of Beat Saber uses SABER files. You can open a SABER file in Beat Saber (Windows) or Open Saber VR (Windows). To use a SABER file with Beat Saber, you must first download and install a plug-in that allows you to import custom sabers. For example, you can download and install Beat Saber Mod Manager and select the Custom Sabers option during installation to use that program to import custom sabers. After you install Beat Saber Mod Manager, a Custom Sabers directory will appear in Beat Sabers Beat Saber directory. Place your SABER files in that directory, and they will appear in-game on the Custom Saber menu. Windows Beat Games Beat Saber. Open Saber VRFree.",
    "Windows Beat Games Beat Saber. Open Saber VR.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".LSL",
    "LiveSplit Layout",
    "4.0",
    "An LSL file is a layout file meant to be used with LiveSplit, a timer program used by video game speedrunners. It contains XML data that alters LiveSplits appearance. LSL files can control LiveSplits size, text formatting, and background, as well as how the program shows a players timer and splits. LSL file open in LiveSplit Speedrunners use LiveSplit to track how quickly they complete video games, sections of video games, and/or specific in-game challenges. They can then compare their times with other speedrunners times, to see if theyve set a new record. When a speedrunner first opens LiveSplit, the program appears as a simple black box with a white timer. Typically, speedrunners spruce up LiveSplits appearance by editing the programs layout. For example, a speedrunner might add a title for their speedrun, show the titles of their splits (which are often loaded from an.LSS file), and change LiveSplits size and background color. To edit LiveSplits layout, speedrunners right-click in the program and select Edit Layout.. The programs Layout Editor then appears. After a speedrunner has finished editing LiveSplits layout, they can save their custom layout by right-clicking in the program and selecting Save Layout or Save Layout As.. The layout will then be saved as an LSL file, which a speedrunner can reload the next time they open LiveSplit. NOTE: LSL files may contain references to.ASL files, which are LiveSplit Auto Splitter Scripts. To open an LSL file in LiveSplit, right-click in the program and select Open Layout → From File.. Then, navigate to and select your LSL file. LiveSplit will then apply the layout settings your LSL file contains. If necessary, you can also open an LSL file in any text editor, such as Microsoft Notepad (Windows), Apple TextEdit (Mac), or GitHub Atom (cross-platform), to edit the XML text it contains. For example, you might want to open and edit your LSL file in a text editor to update a path to an ASL file. Windows LiveSplit. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Linux Vim. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS. Disc label design created with LightScribe Template Labeler, a disc label design program developed by Hewlett-Packard; contains text and/or images that can be burned into the top of a CD or DVD using a LightScribe disc burner. Windows HP LightScribe Template Labeler.",
    "Windows LiveSplit. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Linux Vim. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS. Windows HP LightScribe Template Labeler.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".EIX",
    "Metin2 Game Data File",
    "4.0",
    "Data file used by Metin2, a Massively Multiplayer Online Role-Playing Game (MMORPG) that takes place in an eastern fantasy world; may contain level data, 3D objects, sounds, textures, scripts, and other data used by the game. EIX files are typically saved in the /pack directory along with a corresponding.EPK file. Windows Ymir Entertainment Metin2 —  (Discontinued).+ Metin2 File Extractor.",
    "Windows Ymir Entertainment Metin2 —  (Discontinued).+ Metin2 File Extractor.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".GMS",
    "Garrys Mod Save File",
    "4.0",
    "The GMS file format is a minimal binary format used to organize and store gesture and motion signals. It contains gestures (human motions) that people can use to control computers. The GMS format stores only the minimal features needed to represent movement and gesture information. These include flexible dimensionality for movements, encoded variables, versatile structuration, and spatial and temporal properties. GMS files are supported by the LibGMS library. Windows LibGMS Mac LibGMS Linux LibGMS. A GMS file contains a game save created by Garrys Mod, a physics sandbox game that revolves around building random objects. It stores information about the game progress at the time it was saved, which includes the map state and locations of entities. GMS files can be uploaded to Steam Workshop to share game saves that can be played by other users. When you save game progress in Garrys Mod, a GMS file is created to store the save information. You can save a game by holding Q on the keyboard, which opens the menu, select the Saves tab on the right, and click Save. You can also type the F6 key as a shortcut. GMS files are created by default in the following location in the game installation folder: /​GarrysMod/​garrysmod/​save. Windows Facepunch Studios Garrys Mod. Mac Facepunch Studios Garrys Mod.",
    "Windows LibGMS. Mac LibGMS. Linux LibGMS. Windows Facepunch Studios Garrys Mod. Mac Facepunch Studios Garrys Mod.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".PEX",
    "Compiled Papyrus Script",
    "4.0",
    "Stores data models and reports created with Merak Peep, a petroleum economic evaluation program; used to create What if scenarios, analyze risks and market sensitivity, forecast production levels, and estimate reserves. Windows Merak Peep. Program that can be run within the ProBoard bulletin board system (BBS); written in C/C++ using the software development kit supplied with ProBoard; often less than 50K in size. Windows ProBoard BBS system Linux ProBoard BBS system. A PEX file is the compiled, binary version of a Papyrus script (.PSC file). It contains commands that, when triggered, cause an event to occur in a The Elder Scrolls or Fallout video game, such as Skyrim or Fallout 4. Skyrim and Fallout 4 modders have created tools that allow them to examine, edit, and add to the games PEX files. Papyrus is a scripting language Bethesda created for use in their The Elder Scrolls and Fallout series of video games. Papyrus scripts, which are typically written in PSC source code files and compiled to PEX binary files, are used to control in-game logic and trigger in-game events. Many Elder Scrolls and Fallout games contain Bethesda-developed PEX files. Because Bethesda has given players access to the Papyrus scripting language, players who want to modify the PC versions of Skyrim and Fallout 4 can create their own Papyrus scripts and use them to customize the games behavior. To do so, modders must: Write and save their script as a source code file (usually a PSC file). Use a Papyrus compiler to compile their script to a PEX file. Place the PEX file in Skyrim or Fallout 4s data/​scripts folder. If a modder wishes to modify the script an existing PEX file contains, they must first decompile the file to its editable, source code form. You can use either Champollion (Windows) or PEXInspector (Windows) to decompile a PEX file to a PSC file. Then, you can open and edit the PSC file in any text or source code editor, such as Microsoft Visual Studio Code (cross-platform). How do I compile a PSC file into a PEX file? You can use the Papyrus Script Manager, which is included in the Bethesda Creation Kit, to compile a PSC file into a PEX file. Anyone who owns a PC copy of either Skyrim or Fallout 4 can download the Creation Kit from either Steam or the Bethesda.net Launcher. To compile a PSC file to a PEX file: Place your PSC file in your games data/scripts/source folder. Open the Creation Kit and select Gameplay → Papyrus Script Manager. From the dropdown list that appears, select and right-click your PSC script. Select Compile. Windows Champollion. PEXInspectorFree. Document created by West Publisher, a program used to capture digital artifacts from legal proceedings; stores an exhibit for use in a courtroom hearing; can be distributed standalone or bundled into a.PTZ file for distribution. Windows West Publisher —  (Discontinued). West E-Transcript Bundle ViewerFree.",
    "Windows Merak Peep. Windows ProBoard BBS system. Linux ProBoard BBS system. Windows Champollion. PEXInspector. Windows West Publisher —  (Discontinued). West E-Transcript Bundle Viewer.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".KV3",
    "KeyValues3 Game Data File",
    "4.0",
    "A KV3 file is a plain text data file used by a Valve video game, such as Dota 2 or CounterStrike:Global Offensive (CS:GO). It contains text written in a structure similar to JSON. In CS:GO, KV3 files control how the games bots behave. KV3 file open in GitHub Atom KV3 stands for KeyValues3, which is a Valve-developed data format that is similar to JSON and XML. However, KV3 files also support binary encodings, versioning, and data annotations. They also include support for some additional text syntax. Each KV3 file begins with a line that contains a kv3 encoding header. It then goes on to define how a game object behaves. For example, bt_default.kv3 is a KV3 behavior tree, which defines how CS:GO bots behave in deathmatches. Bt_default.kv3 contains references to other KV3 files, stored in CS:GOs scripts/​ai/​modules directory. Those KV3 files contain scripts that bots execute to perform in-game actions. You can open and edit a KV3 file in any text editor or source code editor, such as Microsoft Visual Studio Code (cross-platform) or GitHub Atom (cross-platform). Windows Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Any text editor Mac Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). Apple TextEdit Included with OS. Any text editor Linux Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). Any text editor Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Any text editor. Mac Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). Apple TextEdit Included with OS. Any text editor. Linux Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). Any text editor. Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".PUP",
    "PlayStation 4 or 5 Update File",
    "4.0",
    "A PUP file is a file or patch used to update the PlayStation 3 (PS3) system software. It may include security patches, bug fixes, and extra operating system features. PUP files are typically named PS3UPDAT.PUP. You can choose to update the PS3 console by downloading the PUP file from the Internet directly to the console. You can also download the PUP file from the Internet to your computer, then transfer the update file using storage media, such as a hard disk or USB storage device. When transferring the PUP file from a storage device to the PS3, save it in the /​PS3/​UPDATE folder. To update the PS3, select Settings → System Update in the console, then press the X button on the controller to begin the update process. NOTE: To extract the contents of a PUP file in Windows, use the PS3 PUP Extractor program. PS3UPDAT.PUP - The standard filename used for PlayStation 3 updates. Windows PS3 PUP ExtractorFree. Installation package used by Puppy Linux, a variant of the Linux operating system designed for ease of use; referred to as a DotPup file; runs an installer when opened; similar to an.EXE or.MSI file used by Windows software installers. The PUP format has largely been replaced by the.PET format with newer versions of Puppy Linux. Linux Puppy LinuxFree. A PUP file is a system update file used by the Sony PlayStation 4 (PS4) or Playstation 5 (PS5). It contains firmware that is meant to be installed on the corresponding game console. PS4 PUP files are named PS4UPDATE.PUP. PS5 PUP files are named PS5UPDATE.PUP. Typically, the PS4 and PS5 automatically update their system software as needed, via a connection to the Internet. However, a user can also manually update their consoles software. To do so, the user must first download the appropriate PUP file from PlayStation.com. You might want to manually update your consoles software if: The automatic update process fails Your console is not connected to the Internet You can also use a PUP file to perform a factory reset on your device. Doing so will overwrite your devices current firmware and delete all the data on your console, including saved game data. To install a PUP file on a PS4 or PS5: Download the appropriate PUP file (PS4UPDATE.PUP or PS5UPDATE.PUP) from PlayStation.com. On a FAT32-formatted USB drive, create a folder named PS4 or PS5. In that folder, create another folder named UPDATE. Place your PUP file in the UPDATE folder. Connect your USB drive to your console. Start your console in Safe Mode. To update your consoles software, select Update System Software. To factory reset your console, select Reinstall System Software. Select Update from USB Storage Device → OK. Windows Meant to be installed on a PlayStation 4 or PlayStation 5 Mac Meant to be installed on a PlayStation 4 or PlayStation 5.",
    "Windows PS3 PUP Extractor. Linux Puppy Linux. Windows Meant to be installed on a PlayStation 4 or PlayStation 5. Mac Meant to be installed on a PlayStation 4 or PlayStation 5.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".XS",
    "Age of Empires 3 Map AI Generator",
    "4.0",
    "File used for generating artificial intelligence (AI) for custom maps in Age of Empires III (AOE3); referenced during the map compilation process when a custom XML map file is loaded. Common XS filenames are aiLoaderStandard.xs and aiMain.xs.. Windows Microsoft Age of Empires 3. Mac MacSoft Age of Empires 3.",
    "Windows Microsoft Age of Empires 3. Mac MacSoft Age of Empires 3.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".PQHERO",
    "Puzzle Quest Saved Game",
    "4.0",
    "Game saved in Puzzle Quest: Challenge of the Warlords, a puzzle-based role playing game (RPG); saves the players progress and game statistics so that gameplay can be resumed at a later time. Puzzle Quest saved games are named [hero name].pqhero. In order to load the saved game on a different computer, the filename must match the name of the hero on the other system. Windows Infinite Interactive Puzzle Quest.",
    "Windows Infinite Interactive Puzzle Quest.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".SLP",
    "Project Slippi Replay",
    "4.0",
    "An SLP file contains a project created by Steinberg SpectraLayers Pro, an advanced audio spectrum editor; supports multiple audio layers with each layer containing independent edits and filters; enables unmixing of standard audio files, sound isolation, and custom sound shaping; used by sound engineers and audio-video editors. SLP files save references to the source media files, layers, edits, and applied filters. SpectraLayers Pro was developed by Sony until it was sold to MAGIX in 2016, and then was acquired by Steinberg in 2019. Windows Steinberg SpectraLayers Pro. Mac Steinberg SpectraLayers Pro. An SLP file is a Super Smash Bros. Melee match replay created by Project Slippi. It contains a replay and statistics about the match shown in the replay. You can play SLP files in the Slippi Launcher desktop app. Project Slippi is a fan-made extension to Super Smash Bros. Melee that adds several features. One of the features Project Slippi adds is the ability to automatically save match replays as SLP files. With this feature enabled, after a match played using Project Slippi concludes, the program automatically saves a replay of that match and the matchs statistics in an SLP file. Players can watch the replay by transferring it to their PC and opening it in Slippi Launcher, which is available for Windows, macOS, and Linux. You can open an SLP file in Slippi Launcher (multiplatform) to play the replay it contains. The easiest way to open an SLP file is to place it in the ~/​Users/​YourUserName/​Slippi directory, select Slippi Launchers Replays tab, and then open your SLP file. Windows Slippi Desktop Launcher. Mac Slippi Desktop Launcher. Linux Slippi Desktop LauncherFree. Project created with with Sisulizer, a visual software localization tool; saved in an XML format and contains the localization project data; stores all project translations and modifications. Windows Sisulizer. An SLP file is a mod file used with Stormworks, an air sea rescue simulation game. It contains data used to modify Stormworkss default gameplay. SLP files are installed using StormLoader, an open-source mod manager for Stormworks. SLP file open in StormLoader Stormworks players use SLP files to add additional vehicles, parts, structures, cosmetic enhancements, and gameplay options to Stormworks. To do so, they download SLP files from Discord, GitHub, or another download source, import the files into StormLoader, and then launch Stormworks from within StormLoader. StormLoader then applies the players selected mods to their copy of Stormworks. Using StormLoader, players can also create separate profiles that include different collections of SLP files. This can be useful if a player wants to switch between several uniquely customized variations of Stormworks. To import an SLP file into StormLoader, select the Add button located in the programs upper left-hand corner. You can import any number of SLP files before launching a modded game of Stormworks (by selecting StormLoaders Launch button). Windows StormLoaderFree.",
    "Windows Steinberg SpectraLayers Pro. Mac Steinberg SpectraLayers Pro. Windows Slippi Desktop Launcher. Mac Slippi Desktop Launcher. Linux Slippi Desktop Launcher. Windows Sisulizer. Windows StormLoader.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".WL3",
    "Wolfenstein 3D Game Data",
    "4.0",
    "A WL3 file is a game data file used by the three-episode version of Wolfenstein 3D, the first game in id Softwares Wolfenstein series. It may contain sprites, textures, sounds, maps, or other game data. Modders sometimes modify the data WL3 files contain, to customize Wolfenstein 3D. iD Software released three versions of Wolfenstein 3D. The first, which was a shareware version that included only one episode, stored game data in.WL1 files. The second, which was a registered version that included three episodes, stored game data in WL3 files. The third, which included six episodes, stored game data in.WL6 files. All versions of Wolfenstein 3D contain nine default game data files. In the three-episode version of Wolfenstein 3D, these files are named: AUDIOHED.wl3 AUDIOT.wl3 CONFIG.wl3 GAMEMAPS.wl3 MAPHEAD.wl3 VGADICT.wl3 VGAGRAPH.wl3 VGAHEAD.wl3 VSWAP.wl3. Various Wolfenstein 3D modding programs allow you to open WL3 files and edit or extract the game data they contain. For example, you can open many WL3 files in SLADE (multiplatform) and Havocs Wolf3D Editor (Windows). Windows id Software Wolfenstein 3D. SLADE. Havocs Wolf3D Editor. Mac id Software Wolfenstein 3D. SLADE. Linux SLADEFree.",
    "Windows id Software Wolfenstein 3D. SLADE. Havocs Wolf3D Editor. Mac id Software Wolfenstein 3D. SLADE. Linux SLADE.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".VRCW",
    "VRChat World",
    "4.0",
    "A VRCW file is a world created for VRChat, a free-to-play virtual reality (VR) platform in which users create custom avatars and socialize with each other within user-generated worlds. It is a Unity AssetBundle that contains all the assets needed to render a VRChat world, which a user created using the Unity game engine and the VRChat Software Development Kit (SDK). Released in 2017, VRChat allows users to travel to unique worlds and socialize with each other using VR headsets, microphones, and custom avatars. VRChat users can do almost anything, including host virtual dance parties, run Dungeons and Dragons sessions, or explore simulated mountain passes, within a VRChat world. VRChat world developers create worlds using the Unity game development engine and VRChats SDK. When a developer has finished creating their world, they can upload it to VRChat as a private world, for use with their friends, or a public world, which will then become available to anyone who uses VRChat. In the process, the world is saved as a VRCW file. VRChats developers and community test user-submitted worlds in the VRChat Community Labs, before releasing them to the general public. As of 2020, VRChat users had created over 50,000 worlds. NOTE: Sometimes, VRChat users steal and re-upload other users worlds, claiming them as their own creations. This practice is generally frowned upon. VRCW files are not meant to be manually opened. However, you can extract a VRCW files assets using Assets Bundle Extractor, uTinyRipper, or DevX-GameRecovery. You can then import those assets into Unity to use them while creating your own VRChat world. How do I upload a VRChat world to VRChat? After youve finished editing your VRChat world in Unity, you can upload it to VRChat for use as a VRChat world. To do so, open the VRChat SDK Build Control Panel (VRChat SDK → Show Control Panel → Builder) in Unity. Then select Publish New Build and follow the on-screen instructions to upload your world. (This process will transform your world into a VRCW file.) How do I access my VRChat worlds in VRChat? To access your VRChat worlds from within VRChat, open the Worlds Menu and select the Mine tab. Your uploaded worlds will appear within the Worlds Menu. Android DevX-GameRecovery. Windows VRChat. uTinyRipper. Unity Assets Bundle Extractor. DevX-GameRecovery. Mac DevX-GameRecovery.",
    "Android DevX-GameRecovery. Windows VRChat. uTinyRipper. Unity Assets Bundle Extractor. DevX-GameRecovery. Mac DevX-GameRecovery.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".CFG",
    "M.U.G.E.N Configuration File",
    "4.0",
    "A CFG file is a generic preference file that stores settings and configuration information. It is used by various programs, which means different CFG files may store data in different formats. CFG files typically should not be opened manually but may be saved in a text format that can be viewed in a text editor. Generic configuration files are often named config.cfg. Text-based CFG files often contain one variable and value per line in the following format: var1=low var2=med var3=high To test if a program automatically generates a default CFG file, move the configuration file out of the default directory when the program is not running. Then open the program and check the directory to see if a new CFG file has been created. Also, a CFG file can sometimes be moved or deleted to revert to a programs default settings. NOTE: Linux configuration files typically use the.CONF extension rather than CFG. Windows File Viewer Plus. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Microsoft WordPad Included with OS. Notepad2. Other text editor Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. MacroMates TextMate. MacVim. Other text editor Android File Viewer for Android. Settings file used by Celestia, a 3D universe simulation program; saved in a plain text format and specifies the data files to load when the program is started; can be edited by the user, but should be done so carefully since improper changes to the file can cause Celestias loading process to fail. Celestia configuration files contain references to.STC,.SSC,.DSC data files,.CEL scripts,.TXF font definitions, and other startup parameters. Windows Celestia. Mac Celestia. Linux CelestiaFree. Three-dimensional model configuration file used by Cal3D, an open source character animation toolkit; saved in a plain text format and contains references to model components; used for putting all model components together. CFG files contain references to Cal3D skeleton files (.CSF or.XSF extension), animation files (.CAF or.XAF extension), meshes (.CMF or.XMF files), and materials (.CRF or.XRF files). They also store the root directory for the referenced files and the model scale. Windows Cal3dViewer. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Any text editor Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. Any text editor Linux Vim. Any text editor. XML keyboard configuration file used by MAME arcade video game emulators; contains keyboard mappings and hotkeys used for playing a game; used for configuring keyboard controls based on the players preferences. Separate CFG files can be saved for different games. This gives players the ability to customize controls separately depending on the game being played. Windows MAME. ExtraMAME. Mac MacMAME MAME OS XFree. Preferences file created by LightWave 3D, a modeling and rendering application for still frames and animation sequences; saves various application configuration options; used for remembering presets for the user. Windows NewTek LightWave 3D. Mac NewTek LightWave 3D. Contains connection information for connecting to a Citrix server; may include hostname, IP address, server port, username, password, etc; multiple CFG files may be used by Citrix client software to connect to various servers. Windows Citrix Access Client Mac Citrix Access Client Linux Citrix Access Client. Game file used by The Battle for Wesnoth, a free turn-based strategy game for Mac, Windows, and Linux; contains source code written in the Wesnoth Markup Language (WML), a proprietary language used for creating scenarios, campaigns, units, and other game content for The Battle for Wesnoth. CFG files can be edited using any text editor. Some WML syntax highlighting plugins have been developed for text editors such as Emacs and Vi. Windows The Battle for Wesnoth. gVim. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Other text editor Mac The Battle for Wesnoth. MacroMates TextMate. Apple TextEdit Included with OS. MacVim. Other text editor Linux The Battle for Wesnoth. GNU Emacs. Vim. Other text editor. A CFG file is a configuration file used by M.U.G.E.N, a free sprite-based game engine used to create 2D fighting games. It contains settings that control a fighting games gameplay, sounds, and appearance. CFG files used with M.U.G.E.N are typically named mugen.cfg. CFG file open in Microsoft Notepad A M.U.G.E.N CFG file allows fighting game creators to modify their games basic rules, including round time, game difficulty, game speed, combo damage multipliers, and more. It also allows creators to choose what resolution M.U.G.E.N shows their game at, as well as whether M.U.G.E.N should play sound effects and music. Advanced M.U.G.E.N users can customize a number of additional game-related settings using a CFG file. M.U.G.E.Ns default CFG file, mugen.cfg, is stored in the programs data folder. While you can edit game settings directly within mugen.cfg, its recommended that you create a copy of the file before editing it, so you can easily revert M.U.G.E.N to its original settings if necessary. mugen.cfg - The default configuration file included with M.U.G.E.N. It is located in the programs data folder. Because they are plain text documents, you can open mugen.cfg or any other M.U.G.E.N CFG file with any text editor. In Windows, you can open a CFG file with Microsoft Notepad or Microsoft WordPad. In macOS, you can use Apple TextEdit. Windows Elecbyte M.U.G.E.N. Any text editor Mac Any text editor Linux Any text editor.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Microsoft WordPad Included with OS. Notepad2. Other text editor. Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. MacroMates TextMate. MacVim. Other text editor. Android File Viewer for Android. Windows Celestia. Mac Celestia. Linux Celestia. Windows Cal3dViewer. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Any text editor. Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. Any text editor. Linux Vim. Any text editor. Windows MAME. ExtraMAME. Mac MacMAME MAME OS X. Windows NewTek LightWave 3D. Mac NewTek LightWave 3D. Windows Citrix Access Client. Mac Citrix Access Client. Linux Citrix Access Client. Windows The Battle for Wesnoth. gVim. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Other text editor. Mac The Battle for Wesnoth. MacroMates TextMate. Apple TextEdit Included with OS. MacVim. Other text editor. Linux The Battle for Wesnoth. GNU Emacs. Vim. Other text editor. Windows Elecbyte M.U.G.E.N. Any text editor. Mac Any text editor. Linux Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".BSA",
    "Bethesda Softworks Archive",
    "4.0",
    "Compressed game data archive used by Bethesda Softworks video games; contains 3d models, textures, sounds, and other data referenced by each game. BSA files are not meant to be altered, but they can be modded in order to change the gameplay. Various programs exist to unpack and alter BSA files. Windows BSA Commander. Fallout mod manager. FO3Archive. Bethesda Archive ExtractorFree.",
    "Windows BSA Commander. Fallout mod manager. FO3Archive. Bethesda Archive Extractor.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".MHR",
    "Mega Hack v7 Pro Macro",
    "4.0",
    "An MHR file is a macro created by Mega Hack v7 Pro, a modding and botting tool for Geometry Dash. It contains a recording of the actions a player performed during a Geometry Dash level playthrough. Players typically create MHR files while creating Geometry Dash botrun videos. Geometry Dash is a multiplatform game in which players click in time with music to jump a cube over obstacles. Mega Hack v7 Pro is a modding tool available for the Windows version of Geometry Dash. Among other things, Mega Hack v7 Pro allows players to control the games speed and record their game actions as a macro. They can then replay their macro at normal (or faster) speed to create level speedrun videos, which are often referred to as botrun or bot videos (because the player is not manually controlling their cube during these videos). Optionally, players can save the Mega Hack v7 Pro macros they create as MHR files (using the Save option found in the apps Macros section). This allows them to share the macros theyve created with other Mega Hack v7 Pro users. You can open an MHR file with Mega Hack v7 Pro (Windows). To do so, load the file in the apps Macros section. Windows Mega Hack v7 Pro.",
    "Windows Mega Hack v7 Pro.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".FPS",
    "FPS Creator Segment File",
    "4.0",
    "File created by FPS Creator, a program that allows game developers to create First Person Shooter (FPS) games; contains one or more segments, which are building blocks used to create levels. Windows The Game Creators FPS CreatorFree.",
    "Windows The Game Creators FPS Creator.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".AGE3XSAV",
    "Age of Empires 3: The WarChiefs Save File",
    "4.0",
    "Save file used by Age of Empires 3: The WarChiefs, an expansion pack for the real-time strategy game series; contains game progress, such as resources, buildings, and settlers. AGE3XSAV files are located in the /​My Documents/​My Games/​Age of Empires 3/​Savegame/​ directory. The Age of Empires 3 game without expansion packs uses the.AGE3SAV extension for save files. The second expansion, Age of Empires 3: The Asian Dynasties, uses the.AGE3YSAV file extension. Windows Microsoft Age of Empires 3.",
    "Windows Microsoft Age of Empires 3.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".ZS7",
    "ZSNES Slot 7 Saved State File",
    "4.0",
    "Save state file created by ZSNES, an SNES emulator program for PCs; contains an emulated save state snapshot of the game progress; may be used to save state at any point during gameplay. ZSNES provides numerous save states from 0 to 99 (file extensions.ZS0,.ZS1,.ZS2, and so on). To change the save state slot, use the Pick State option under the Game menu, or press the F3 key and use the arrow keys. NOTE: A ZSNES save state is different than an in-game save state. A game may only allow a user to save state at specific game milestones whereas ZSNES save states can be created at any time during gameplay. Windows ZSNES. Linux ZSNESFree.",
    "Windows ZSNES. Linux ZSNES.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".BVS",
    "BVS Solitaire Collection Saved Game",
    "4.0",
    "A BVS file is a saved game file created by BVS Solitaire Collection, an application that allows users to play over 500 types of solitaire-style card games. It contains an in-progress game saved by a user. BVS files are used to resume paused games and share in-progress games with other BVS Solitaire Collection players. BVS file open in BVS Solitaire Collection for Mac BVS Solitaire Collection is a cross-platform application that allows solitaire enthusiasts to play not only classic solitaire, but Tri-Peaks, FreeCell, Spider Solitaire, and many additional variations. Players can even use the programs Modify Rules feature to create their own, custom versions of solitaire. They can also use BVS Solitaire Collections statistics- and score-tracking features to see whether their play is improving and how they stack up against other players from around the world. When a player saves a game in BVS Solitaire Collection, the program creates a BVS file that stores the games current state. The player can use this file to resume playing their in-progress game at a later time, return to an earlier juncture within their current game, or share their game with another BVS Solitaire Collection player. NOTE: Players must save BVS files with the default filename assigned by BVS Solitaire Collection. Attempting to change a BVS files default name may cause BVS Solitaire Collection to freeze. BVS Solitaire Collection players can open BVS files by selecting Game → Open Game.. from the programs menu bar. After opening the BVS file, the in-progress solitaire game it contains will appear in BVS Solitaire Collection. Windows BVS Solitaire Collection. Mac BVS Solitaire Collection. iOS BVS Solitaire Collection for iOS.",
    "Windows BVS Solitaire Collection. Mac BVS Solitaire Collection. iOS BVS Solitaire Collection for iOS.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".ZS6",
    "ZSNES Slot 6 Saved State File",
    "4.0",
    "Save state file created by ZSNES, an SNES emulator program; contains a snapshot save state from an emulated SNES game; used for saving and loading game progress; may be used to save state at any point during gameplay. ZSNES provides numerous save states from 0 to 99 (file extensions.ZS0,.ZS1,.ZS2, and so on). To change the save state slot file that is used, press the F3 key and use the arrow keys to navigate to the desired state. NOTE: A ZSNES save state is not the same as an in-game save state. A game may only allow a user to save state at specific game milestones whereas ZSNES save states can be created at any time during gameplay. Windows ZSNES. Linux ZSNESFree.",
    "Windows ZSNES. Linux ZSNES.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".ZS3",
    "ZSNES Slot 3 Saved State File",
    "4.0",
    "Save stat file created by ZSNES, an SNES emulator for PCs; stores a saved game snapshot for an emulated game; used to save and load game progress. ZSNES provides numerous save states from 0 to 99 (file extensions.ZS0,.ZS1,.ZS2, and so on). NOTE: ZSNES save states are different than in-game save states since ZSNES save states can be created at any point during gameplay. In-game save states typically are controlled by milestones within the game content. Windows ZSNES. Linux ZSNESFree.",
    "Windows ZSNES. Linux ZSNES.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".SUB_LOC",
    "Submachine Universe Location",
    "4.0",
    "A SUB_LOC file is a modified Macromedia Flash Player Compressed Movie (.SWF file) used in Submachine Universe, a single-player exploration game developed by Mateusz Skutnik. It contains an interactive animation representing a location within the game. SUB_LOC files are stored within Submachine Universes SUB_LOC folder. The Submachine series of games consists of fifteen point-and-click adventure games developed by Mateusz Skutnik. Submachine Universe was first released in 2009 and received updates through 2017, when the game was re-released on itch.io. When players download Submachine Universe, they receive a.ZIP package containing a note from the developer, the games executable (.EXE) file, its introductory.swf animation, and a SUB_LOC folder containing SUB_LOC files. Each SUB_LOC file corresponds to a location that can be explored and interacted with within the game. NOTE: Submachine Universes SUB_LOC folder and SUB_LOC files must be stored in the same directory as its executable file (subnet_pc.exe) for the game to work properly. Because SUB_LOC files are not standard SWF files, they cannot be played in Adobe Flash Player or other typical SWF players. At this time, there is no known program that opens SUB_LOC files, outside of Submachine Universe. No programs available.",
    "No programs available.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".SG0",
    "Humongous Entertainment Game Save File",
    "4.0",
    "Game file created and used by Humongous Entertainment games, such as Backyard Baseball and the Pajama Sam series; stores saved progress of your game; referenced by the game when continuing a saved game. SG0 files are used by Humongous Entertainment games that use the Script Utility for Maniac Mansion (SCUMM) engine. The file is located with other game data files in the saved game directory path. Windows Humongous Entertainment Pajama Sams Sock Works. Mac Humongous Entertainment Pajama Sams Sock Works. Linux Humongous Entertainment Pajama Sams Sock Works.",
    "Windows Humongous Entertainment Pajama Sams Sock Works. Mac Humongous Entertainment Pajama Sams Sock Works. Linux Humongous Entertainment Pajama Sams Sock Works.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".AGX",
    "AGT Game File",
    "4.0",
    "Game file used by Adventure Game Toolkit (AGT) interpreters such as AGiliTy; contains a game where you navigate through a story by typing commands; similar to.ZLB and.BLB Interactive Fiction (IF) game files. AGT games are packaged with a variety of file extensions, such as.INS,.TTL, and DA1, that provide certain functions for the game. These files can be converted into a single AGX file with the AGT2AGX utility that comes with AGiliTy. This conversion reduces clutter, decreases load time, and increases portability. Windows AGiliTy. Mac Spatterlight. Linux AGiliTyFree.",
    "Windows AGiliTy. Mac Spatterlight. Linux AGiliTy.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".PROFILE",
    "Godot Engine Feature Profile File",
    "4.0",
    "Configuration file used by Citrix XenApp, a software package used for delivering virtualized software to client systems; stores the data needed for launching a streamed application to the user. PROFILE files can be edited using the Citrix Streaming Profiler program included with XenApp software. Windows Citrix XenApp. Shell profile used by Linux and Mac OS X terminal programs; contains definitions for a shell environment, such as environment variables, scripts to execute, and other instructions; used for storing pre-defined settings that are loaded when a shell program starts. When opening Apple Terminal in Mac OS X, the program automatically searches for a PROFILE file and executes it line by line as a shell script. To manually run a PROFILE file, use the command source ~/.profile. PROFILE files are hidden files that do not have a filename prefix. They are always named.profile and are located in the users home directory. NOTE: Apple Terminal is a Bash shell program. Mac Apple Terminal Included with OS. MacroMates TextMate. GNU Bash. Linux GNU BashFree. Player profile saved by one of the Pandemic Studios third-person shooter games; contains player information, such as the profile name, player preferences, and progress in campaign missions; saved to the /​GameData/​SaveGames/​ directory of a game installation. PROFILE files apply to Pandemic Studios games such as Star Wars: Battlefront 2, Mercenaries 2, and The Sabateur. Windows Pandemic Studios Star Wars: Battlefront II Pandemic Studios Mercenaries 2: World in Flames Pandemic Studios The Sabateur. A PROFILE file is a settings file created by Godot Engine, an open-source game engine. It stores a list of features that configure how the Editor appears in Godot Engine, such as enabled panels, menus, buttons, and 2D or 3D engine requirements. PROFILE files are saved in a JSON-like text format. PROFILE file open in Godot Engine 3.2.1 Godot Engine is an open-source game engine used to create 2D and 3D games for various platforms, such as Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, iOS, and the web. Godot users can export Editor feature profiles to save the settings of the Editor for the various types of projects they work on. Users can create a PROFILE with Godot Engine by selecting Editor → Manage Editor Features.., then clicking Export. They can then import the PROFILE to configure the Editor with their favorite presets. NOTE: PROFILE files were introduced with the release of Godot Engine 3.2.1. You can open a Godot PROFILE file with Godot Engine in Windows, macOS, or Linux. To open the PROFILE with Godot Engine, select Editor → Manage Editor Features.., then click Import. Since PROFILE files are saved in plain text format, you can also open them with a text editor, such as Microsoft Notepad in Windows or Apple TextEdit in macOS. Windows Godot Engine. Any text editor Mac Godot Engine. Any text editor Linux Godot Engine. Any text editor.",
    "Windows Citrix XenApp. Mac Apple Terminal Included with OS. MacroMates TextMate. GNU Bash. Linux GNU Bash. Windows Pandemic Studios Star Wars: Battlefront II Pandemic Studios Mercenaries 2: World in Flames Pandemic Studios The Sabateur. Windows Godot Engine. Any text editor. Mac Godot Engine. Any text editor. Linux Godot Engine. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".INFO",
    "Minecraft Forge Information File",
    "4.0",
    "An INFO file is a formatted information document created by Texinfo, a GNU Project help document generation tool. It stores documentation in a book format with chapters, sections, and subsections and can contain up to four layers of depth. INFO documents are used for storing software help manuals and technical books. They can be viewed with an Info viewer, such as Texinfos Info reader or GNU Emacs. Linux GNU Texinfo. GNU EmacsFree. An INFO file is a generic text information file that may be included with various software programs or file downloads. It contains unformatted plain text that describes the contents of the software or download. INFO files are often used to document version information, authorship, and copyright information. Since INFO files are saved in a text format, you can use a simple text editor to open an INFO file. Microsoft Notepad or WordPad can be used in Windows, Apple TextEdit can be used in macOS, and gedit can be used in Linux. Windows Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Microsoft WordPad Included with OS. Notepad++. Other text editor Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. MacroMates TextMate. Other text editor Linux gedit. GNU Emacs. Other text editor. An INFO file is a data file used by Minecraft Forge, a tool that enables users to install Minecraft mods. It contains plain text information in JSON format that describes one or more mods and their properties. NOTE: The file is stored within a.JAR or.ZIP archive that can be unzipped by programs such as 7-Zip and WinRAR. Since the INFO file contains plain text it can be edited by any text editor. mcmod.info - A common name for the INFO that describes one or more mods. Windows Minecraft ForgeFree. File used by Drupal, a website content management system; stores information about a Drupal module (.MODULE file), which is a plugin that adds features to the base Drupal installation; contains the module name, description, the core Drupal version supported by the module, and any dependencies required by the module. An INFO file with the same filename prefix is required in order for Drupal to recognize the module. For example mymodule.module and mymodule.info are valid names. Both files should be installed to the following directory, where mysite is your website (which could also be the all directory): sites/​mysite/​modules/​. Windows Drupal. Any text editor Mac Drupal. Any text editor Linux Drupal. Any text editor. Stores information about images saved in a Zoom Browser photo album; includes thumbnail image data, which enables quick browsing of images in the album; created when one or more image files are opened by ZoomBrowser. The ZoomBrowser index file is typically named Zbthumbnail.info and is saved in the same directory as the images it corresponds to. The Zbthumbnail.info file is usually hidden but may appear inside your album folder depending on your computers file viewing settings. NOTE: Canon ZoomBrowser EX is no longer available for download. Windows Canon ZoomBrowser EX.",
    "Linux GNU Texinfo. GNU Emacs. Windows Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. Microsoft WordPad Included with OS. Notepad++. Other text editor. Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. MacroMates TextMate. Other text editor. Linux gedit. GNU Emacs. Other text editor. Windows Minecraft Forge. Windows Drupal. Any text editor. Mac Drupal. Any text editor. Linux Drupal. Any text editor. Windows Canon ZoomBrowser EX.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".DSG",
    "Doom Saved Game",
    "4.0",
    "Save file created and used by Chocolate Doom, a fan-made version of the Windows 95 Doom; contains saved game progress, which includes the game version, game skill, episode of the game, current level, time since the level began, amount of possessed armor, armor type, attained powers and weapons, whether the player is alive, dead, or respawning, and other game information. NOTE: There are six slots available for saved games and each slot uses a DSG file that is numbered from 0 to 5. The file is named doomsave[slot number].dsg. Windows Chocolate Doom. Mac Chocolate DoomFree. File created by Control Studio, a program used to simulate and prototype electronic systems; stores electronic components and connections on a design canvas; may also store written text and images; used for saving and loading designs, which can be simulated in the software. The Control Studio Gallery window contains electronic components for inputs, processes, amplifiers, drivers, outputs, and measures. Users can select components from within these categories and place them on the design canvas. Windows New Wave Concepts Control Studio. A DSG file contains an embroidery design created by Sierra Stitch Era, a program used to design embroideries stitched with a sewing machine. It stores a vector design that has been drawn from scratch or a scanned image. DSG files also include advanced stitch information, such as fill patterns and thread colors. You can create a DSG file in Stitch Era by selecting File → New or New from the toolbar, and then clicking File → Save. You can open a DSG file in Stitch Era by selecting File → Open or Open from the toolbar. Windows Sierra Stitch Era. File saved by Decker, a game where players assume the role of a hacker that breaks into corporate computers; contains game information such as accepted and completed jobs and inventories; used for storing game progress. NOTE: The Decker program is no longer available to download. Windows Decker.",
    "Windows Chocolate Doom. Mac Chocolate Doom. Windows New Wave Concepts Control Studio. Windows Sierra Stitch Era. Windows Decker.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".UT4MOD",
    "Unreal Tournament 2004 Module",
    "4.0",
    "Module used by Unreal Tournament 2004 (UT2004), a multi-player first-person shooter game; extends UT2004 gameplay with new maps, characters, spawn points, weapon modifications, and graphics; may be loaded into UT2004 by double-clicking the module file. UT2004 allows users to play in team-based or every man for himself modes in a variety of included or added module battlegrounds. Windows Epic Games Unreal Tournament 2004 Mac Epic Games Unreal Tournament 2004 Linux Epic Games Unreal Tournament 2004.",
    "Windows Epic Games Unreal Tournament 2004. Mac Epic Games Unreal Tournament 2004. Linux Epic Games Unreal Tournament 2004.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".STENCYL",
    "Stencyl Game File",
    "4.0",
    "Game file created and used by Stencyl, a cross-platform game creation program that supports Windows, OS X, Linux, iOS, Android, and Flash games; contains an exported game and its resources, which includes scenes, backgrounds, tilesets, sounds, actor types, scene behaviors, and actor behaviors. NOTE: To create the STENCYL file, select File → Export Game.. To import the STENCYL file, select File → Import Game... Windows Stencyl. Mac Stencyl. Linux Stencyl.",
    "Windows Stencyl. Mac Stencyl. Linux Stencyl.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".ISS",
    "FunCom ISS Audio File",
    "4.0",
    "An ISS file is a script used by Inno Setup, a free program used to create Windows program installers. It contains a series of plain text commands that specify where and how a Windows program is installed. ISS files are compiled to create.EXE files. ISS file open in Inno Setup Compiler 6.1 Inno Setup is an InstallShield alternative that allows developers to create Windows installers. To create an installer, Inno Setup compiles the installer instructions an ISS script contains into an EXE file. The instructions specified in an ISS script control all aspects of the related programs installation, including what files are installed and what folders the files are placed in. An ISS file may also include instructions that allow an installer to: Check for prerequisite programs and platform requirements Install or update Windows Registry entries Create an uninstaller Create a desktop shortcut and add a program to a users Start menu Start an application immediately after installing it How do I compile an ISS script into a Windows installer? To compile an ISS file into an EXE file, open the ISS file in Inno Setup and then select Build → Compile from Inno Setups menu bar. You can open and edit an ISS file (Inno Setup Script) in Inno Setup (Windows). To open an ISS file in Inno Setup, select File → Open.. from the programs menu bar. Then, navigate to and open your ISS file. If necessary, you can also open and edit an ISS file in any text editor, including Microsoft Notepad (Windows), Apple TextEdit (Mac), or GitHub Atom (cross-platform). Windows File Viewer Plus. Jrsoftware Inno Setup. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Linux Vim. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS. File used by InstallShield, a program used for creating Windows installers; contains a series of installation settings in a text format; used to submit automated responses during the software installation process so users do not have to enter information into an installation wizard. ISS files are similar to.INI files. Windows Flexera InstallShield. An ISS file is an audio file used by The Longest Journey and other Funcom Oslo A/S video games. It contains music, sound effects, or dialogue that is played within a game. ISS files cannot be played by most popular media players, so if you want to play the files outside the game in which they are used, you must convert them to a standard audio format. Funcom Oslo A/S is a Norwegian video game developer that was founded in 1993 and has produced over 50 games. The Longest Journey, which was produced in 1999, is one of Funcoms most popular and well-known games. It is a point-and-click puzzle-solving adventure game that follows the story of April Ryan, a struggling art student who must navigate between and save two parallel universes. Because ISS files are so uncommon, most popular media players cannot play them. However, you can convert ISS files to a usable audio format using FFmpeg and then play the converted file using a media player such as VLC media player. Windows File Viewer Plus. FFmpeg. Mac FFmpeg. Linux FFmpegFree.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. Jrsoftware Inno Setup. Microsoft Notepad Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Mac Apple TextEdit Included with OS. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Linux Vim. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS. Windows Flexera InstallShield. Windows File Viewer Plus. FFmpeg. Mac FFmpeg. Linux FFmpeg.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".GDG",
    "GDevelop Project File",
    "4.0",
    "A GDG file is a project file used by GDevelop, an open source program used to create games without programming knowledge. It contains information about a game such as events, objects, and scenes. The file allows you to save and resume your progress when developing a game. The GDevelop application is designed for all types of users that want to create a 2D game without extensive programming knowledge. You can illustrate scenes, add and animate objects, and define events that determine the logic of your gameplay. Windows GDevelop. Mac GDevelop. Linux GDevelopFree.",
    "Windows GDevelop. Mac GDevelop. Linux GDevelop.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".APC",
    "CRYO Interactive APC Audio",
    "4.0",
    "An APC file is an audio file used by a CRYO Interactive Entertainment video game. It contains music, sound effects, or dialogue that is played within a game. APC files cannot be played by most popular media players, so if you want to play the files outside the game in which they are used, you may need to convert them to a standard audio format. In 1990, a group of game developers from French publisher ERE Informatique left that company to form CRYO Interactive Entertainment. CRYO released a number of popular video games, including Dune, Black Moon Chronicles, and Atlantis: The Lost Tales, before filing for bankruptcy in 2002. Atlantis: The Lost Tales used APC files, as did Egypt II, Odyssey: The Search For Ulysses, China: The Forbidden City, and a handful of other CRYO games. If youve encountered an APC file, it likely came from one of CRYO Interactives video games. Because APC files are so uncommon, most popular media players cannot play them. However, you can play APC files using VLC media player, and you can convert APC files to a more usable audio format using FFmpeg. Windows File Viewer Plus. VideoLAN VLC media player. FFmpeg. Mac VideoLAN VLC media player. FFmpeg. Linux VideoLAN VLC media player. FFmpegFree.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. VideoLAN VLC media player. FFmpeg. Mac VideoLAN VLC media player. FFmpeg. Linux VideoLAN VLC media player. FFmpeg.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".KODU2",
    "Kodu World",
    "4.0",
    "A KODU2 file is a game world developed in Microsoft Kodu Game Lab, an educational 3D game development environment. It contains the worlds terrain, placed objects, and tile-based code. Developers export worlds as KODU2 files to share them with others. Kodu Game Lab is a game development program used to teach children about programming. For example, a teacher might use Kodu as part of a middle school game development curriculum. To create levels in Kodu, developers create a world, place game objects, and program those game objects using a tile-based programming language. Developers can export their finished Kodu games, comprised of one more levels, as.KODU files. Developers can also export a single level as a.KODU2 file. Exporting a single level as a KODU2 file can be useful if, for example, a developer needs to turn in only a single level for a homework assignment. It can also be useful if the developer has a question about the code contained in the level, and wants to share only that level with another user. You can open a KODU2 file in Kodu Game Lab (Windows). To do so, double-click the file (on a computer with Kodu installed). The world your KODU2 file contains will then appear in Kodus Load World menu. (If you already have Kodu open, you may have to return to the Main Menu before the world appears in your Load World menu.). Windows Microsoft Kodu Game LabFree.",
    "Windows Microsoft Kodu Game Lab.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".RTTEX",
    "Robinson Technologies Texture File",
    "4.0",
    "An RTTEX file is a texture file saved in the Robinson Technologies Texture (RTTEX) format. It is used by Growtopia, a game that allows you to build worlds, compose music, choose an occupation, trade items, and earn achievements. RTTEX files store one or more textures for gameplay elements, such as characters, items, and landscape. The RTTEX format is a container that supports various image types including JPEG and PVRTC. It may be compressed using zlib. Windows Growtopia. Mac Growtopia.",
    "Windows Growtopia. Mac Growtopia.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".WRPL",
    "Widelands Saved Game File",
    "4.0",
    "Saved game created by Widelands, an open source strategy game; contains all player moves since the initial starting condition, which is stored in a separate.WGF file (also seen with the.WRPL.WGF extension); used to save and load in-game progress. Windows Widelands Mac Widelands Linux Widelands.",
    "Windows Widelands. Mac Widelands. Linux Widelands.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".RPL",
    "Toribash Replay",
    "4.0",
    "An RPL file is a match replay recorded by Toribash, a multiplatform fighting game developed by Nabi Studios. It contains a (plain text) record of everything that occurred during a match, including the fighters movements and the final score. Players can create RPL files at the end of a match by pressing F on their keyboard. In Toribash, players control the joints of stick-figure-like characters to create custom fighting moves. Players can play against the games AI or other players. They can also practice their moves in free play modes. If a player wants to save a replay of their match or free play session, they can do so by pressing F at the end of the session. Toribash then saves a record of the session as an RPL file. Each RPL file contains plain text data that Toribash uses to replay the match or free play session it describes. Players can share RPL files with each other so they can watch and edit each others replays. (To edit a replay, press E while the replay is playing.) Where does Toribash save RPL files? Toribash typically saves RPL files in its ~/​Toribash/​replay directory. For example, the Windows Steam version of Toribash saves RPL files to the following directory: C:/​Program Files (x86)/​Steam/​steamapps/​common/​Toribash/​replay. You can open an RPL file in Toribash (multiplatform). To do so, place the file in your ~/​Toribash/​replay directory. Then, open Toribash and select Setup → Replays. You should be able to select and play the replay your RPL file contains. You can also open an RPL file in any text editor, to view the plain text data it contains. Windows Nabi Studios Toribash. Any text editor Mac Nabi Studios Toribash. Any text editor Linux Nabi Studios Toribash. Any text editor. Game replay file created by Special Force, an online first-person shooter game; saves a first-person perspective video captured during gameplay; sometimes used to submit replays of cheaters to game authorities. RPL files are saved to the C:/​Program Files/​DFIGAMES/​Dragonfly/​Special Force/​Data/​Save/​ directory. NOTE: DF Interactive Special Force has been discontinued. Windows DF Interactive Special Force. An RPL file contains video data encoded with the ESCAPE codec and uncompressed or compressed audio data stored in the ARMovie multimedia container format. It is used in various computer games including Big Red Racing and the Tomb Raider series. RPL files are primarily used for saving replays of gameplay. All of the games that utilize RPL files are discontinued, with most of them being released in the 1990s. Since the files are no longer used to save replays you will most likely only encounter them if you have installed one of these older Eidos-developed PC games. The RPL files all utilize the same multimedia container but may store video encoded in different variations of the ESCAPE codec. The variations include ESCAPE 122, 124, and 130. NOTE: Eidos Interactive was acquired by Square Enix in 2009. There are few programs that support RPL files since they are rarely used. However, you can open and convert RPL files to more common video formats with FFmpeg, then open the converted file with a media player, such as Microsoft Windows Media Player or VLC media player. Windows File Viewer Plus. FFmpeg. Mac FFmpeg. Linux FFmpegFree. An RPL file is a cinema print master file created using Dolby Atmos Monitor and a Dolby Rendering and Mastering Unit, a professional sound rendering system that is made available to only select, cinema-grade filmmakers. It contains audio that is designed to be included in a film that will be played in a Dolby Atmos theater. RPL files can also be played back in the commercially-available Dolby Atmos Mastering Suite and Dolby Atmos Production Suite, using Dolby Atmos Renderer. Dolby provides certain filmmakers and studios with a specially-licensed computer that is designed specifically to let sound engineers and designers create cinema-grade Dolby Atmos audio tracks. These computers, called Dolby Rendering and Mastering Units, are controlled from a secondary PC using Avid Pro Tools, a separately-licensed digital audio workstation, and the Dolby Atmos Monitor. The Monitor is used to condense all the audio files and other information included within a movies Pro Tools project into one master audio track, which is saved with the.rpl extension. This RPL file is then included within a movie before that movie is shipped to and played at Dolby Atmos theaters. While non-cinema-level filmmakers cannot create RPL files, they can play them. Dolbys commercially-available Atmos Mastering Suite and Atmos Production Suite can both play RPL files, using the Dolby Atmos Renderer included within those suites. You can open and play an RPL file with Dolby Atmos Renderer, the audio rendering application included within the Dolby Atmos Mastering Suite and Dolby Atmos Production Suite. To do so, open the Renderer and select File → Open Master File. You can also convert an RPL file to a more usable format, such as WAV, with the Dolby Atmos Conversion Tool included within Dolby Atmos Mastering Suite. Mac Dolby Atmos Mastering Suite. Dolby Atmos Production Suite.",
    "Windows Nabi Studios Toribash. Any text editor. Mac Nabi Studios Toribash. Any text editor. Linux Nabi Studios Toribash. Any text editor. Windows DF Interactive Special Force. Windows File Viewer Plus. FFmpeg. Mac FFmpeg. Linux FFmpeg. Mac Dolby Atmos Mastering Suite. Dolby Atmos Production Suite.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".WPG",
    "Ironsight Game Data File",
    "4.0",
    "A WPG file is a game data file used by Aeria Games IronSight, a first-person military shooter video game. It contains information that describes an aspect of gameplay, such as a weapon, weapon sound, map, character, or character sound. You will most likely only see a WPG file if you are looking to modify Ironsight gameplay. WPG files can be found in the Ironsight game installation directory: C:/​AeriaGames/​Ironsight/​data. weapon_ar.wpg - WPG file containing information about the assault rifle weapon in Ironsight. Windows Aeria Games Ironsight —  (Discontinued).+. A WPG file is a graphic saved in the Corel WordPerfect Graphic (WPG) format. It may store both vector and bitmap data but is primarily a vector graphic used for clipart images. WPG files may include up to 256 colors and may also contain Encapsulated PostScript (.EPS) information. WPG file open in Corel WordPerfect 2021 The WPG format is used primarily by the WordPerfect, Quattro Pro, and Presentations applications included in the WordPerfect Office suite. WPG files come packaged with the suite and are located in the following directory: C:/​Program Files (x86)/​Corel/​WordPerfect Office 2021/​Graphics/​ClipArt You can also create custom WPG graphics with various Corel applications, including CorelDRAW (packaged with the CorelDRAW Graphics Suite). To create a WPG file with CorelDRAW, select File → Export and choose WPG - Corel WordPerfect Graphic from the Save as type dropdown list. You can open WPG files with various Corel applications, such as Corel WordPerfect Office applications (Windows), CorelDRAW Graphics Suite (Windows), and Corel PaintShop Pro (Windows). You can also open WPG files and convert them to other formats with free programs, such as Inkscape (multiplatform), Calligra Karbon, which is included with Calligra Suite (multiplatform), and Lemkesoft GraphicConverter (macOS). The Libwpg project also provides a free C++ library for converting WPG graphics to other formats. Windows Corel WordPerfect 2021. Corel Presentations 2021. Corel Quattro Pro 2021. CorelDRAW Graphics Suite 2023. Corel PaintShop Pro 2023. Canvas GFX Canvas X Pro. Calligra Suite. Inkscape. Mac Lemkesoft GraphicConverter. Inkscape. Calligra Suite. Linux Inkscape. Calligra SuiteFree.",
    "Windows Aeria Games Ironsight —  (Discontinued).+. Windows Corel WordPerfect 2021. Corel Presentations 2021. Corel Quattro Pro 2021. CorelDRAW Graphics Suite 2023. Corel PaintShop Pro 2023. Canvas GFX Canvas X Pro. Calligra Suite. Inkscape. Mac Lemkesoft GraphicConverter. Inkscape. Calligra Suite. Linux Inkscape. Calligra Suite.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".KWN",
    "Game Level Asset Archive",
    "4.0",
    "A KWN file is an archive used in an Étranges Libellules video game, such as Asterix & Obelix XXL. It contains the assets (models, animations, textures, and sounds) used in a specific game level. Étranges Libellules was a French video game developer that released a handful of games, including Asterix & Obelix XXL, Asterix at the Olympic Games, and How to Train Your Dragon. To develop their games, Étranges Libellules used a game development engine called the K engine. Games developed using the K engine store each levels assets in a level-specific KWN archive. If an asset is used across multiple levels, a copy of that asset is stored in each levels KWN file. KWN files are not meant to be opened by players. However, players interested in examining the assets that KWN files contain can do so using AdrienTDs ELB Tools (Windows). Windows ELB ToolsFree.",
    "Windows ELB Tools.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".GRF",
    "Transport Tycoon Graphics Resource File",
    "4.0",
    "Compressed file archive used by Ragnarok Online, a Korean multiplayer game; includes compressed images, sounds, and other data files loaded by Ragnarok. The contents of a Ragnarok GRF file can be browsed and extracted using GRF Factory, a free utility available at neatocool.com. data.grf and sdata.grf - Common names for Ragnarok GRF files that are installed with other game files. Windows GRAVITY Ragnarok Online. GRF Factory. 2D or 3D graph created by DPlot, a program used for plotting scientific data; saves the graph type and associated data; used for distribution plots, wireframe meshes, 3D projections, polar charts, probability charts, triangle plots, hydraulic scales, and many other types of graphs. DPlot is primarily used by scientists, engineers, and other technical professionals. The graphs are often included in presentations and scientific journal articles. NOTE: GRF files can be viewed with the free DPlot Viewer. Windows Hydesoft Computing DPlot. Hydesoft Computing DPlot ViewerFree. Data transformation file created by Javlin CloverDX Designer, an Eclipse-based data integration application; saved using XML formatting and contains a diagram of data readers, writers, transformers, and other data manipulators; used for data integration, cleansing, migration, and warehousing; can be run on a desktop computer for smaller jobs or on a cluster of CloverDX servers for larger jobs. The CloverDX suite includes several editions of the software, including Community, Desktop, Enterprise, Cluster, and OEM versions. The GRF files are created using the CloverDX Designer, which is included with each software edition. The Eclipse IDE is a free Java development application and CloverDX Desktop is a set of proprietary Eclipse plugins that allow CloverDX projects and GRF files to be created. NOTE: Javlin CloverDX was formerly known as Javlin CloverETL. Windows Javlin CloverDX. Mac Javlin CloverDX. Linux Javlin CloverDX. File created by Grapher, an application used for making 2D and 3D graphs; can store one of many different kinds of graphs, such as a line, scatter, or bar graph; also supports advanced plotting types such as a ternary, box-whisker, and hi-low-close plots. Graphs created in Grapher are used for creating technical graphs for publication and presentations. NOTE: GRF files do not contain the source data used to generate the graph. They only store the rendered graph and parameters required for scaling. This differs from.GPJ files, which contain the data as well as the rendered graph. Windows Golden Software Grapher. Resource file used by the Transport Tycoon (TT) and Transport Tycoon Deluxe (TTD) railroad business simulation games; contains a collection of sprites, which store the 2D graphics for game objects; used for displaying and animating railroad vehicles, scenery, and other game objects. The original version of GRF files supported simple sprites. However, the new open source variants of Transport Tycoon, including OpenTTD and TTDPatch, support new graphics data in GRF files. OpenTTDs new format, called NewGRF, can include data for vehicles, cargo, buildings, railroad types, and industries. OpenTTD provides GRF game packages, which are downloadable NewGRF files that can be applied to the game to display custom graphics and to alter game properties. Windows Transport Tycoon —  (Discontinued). OpenTTD. GRFCodec. Linux OpenTTD. GRFCodecFree. Graph created by a number of different possible programs, including Micrografx Graph Plus, GraphEdit, and Accelrys Insight II. Windows Graph Edit Other graphing program. A GRF file is a game data file used by a Minecraft: Legacy Console Edition game. It contains rules that Minecraft executes in a game world, to show messages and control how players and items spawn. Minecraft: Legacy Console Editions tutorial and mini-game worlds use GRF files. Minecraft: Legacy Console Edition is gamers collective name for console versions of Minecraft developed by 4J Studios. These versions of Minecraft have been replaced by Minecraft Bedrock Edition. The 4J Studios versions of Minecraft include many proprietary game data files, which other versions of Minecraft do not use. These include GRF files. Each GRF file specifies certain events and settings applied to a default Minecraft: Legacy Console Edition world (.MCS file). For example, one GRF file specifies the initial messages, health, hunger, and items a player receives in the games tutorial world. Other GRF files specify when chests refill and where player spawn points are located in mini-game worlds. You can open a GRF file with Universal Minecraft Tool (Windows), MCCToolChest (Windows), or PCK Studio (Windows). Doing so allows you to edit the rules the GRF file contains. Windows Pryze Software Universal Minecraft Tool. MCCToolChest. PCK StudioFree.",
    "Windows GRAVITY Ragnarok Online. GRF Factory. Windows Hydesoft Computing DPlot. Hydesoft Computing DPlot Viewer. Windows Javlin CloverDX. Mac Javlin CloverDX. Linux Javlin CloverDX. Windows Golden Software Grapher. Windows Transport Tycoon —  (Discontinued). OpenTTD. GRFCodec. Linux OpenTTD. GRFCodec. Windows Graph Edit Other graphing program. Windows Pryze Software Universal Minecraft Tool. MCCToolChest. PCK Studio.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".BYML",
    "Binary YAML File",
    "4.0",
    "A BYML file is a binary file that contains YAML data. It is used to control configuration settings in a Nintendo video game. BYML files are used in several popular games, including The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild, Splatoon 2, Super Mario Odyssey, and Animal Crossing: New Horizons. YAML (YAML Aint Markup Language) is a human-readable data format used for data serialization.YAML files are most often used as configuration files, which define an applications settings. Some Nintendo video games use YAML files that have been converted to a binary format, which are saved as BYML files. These files control in-game settings and characteristics, such as what levels are available to select. NOTE: BYML files may also be saved with the.baischedule,.baniminfo,.bgdata,.bgsvdata,.bquestpack,.mubin, or.byaml extension. BYML files are binary files that are typically not meant to be opened. However, you can convert a BYML file to a YAML file using byml-v2 (cross-platform), a fan-created BYML conversion tool. Then, you can examine and edit the YAML data your BYML file contains. Windows byml-v2. Mac byml-v2. Linux byml-v2Free.",
    "Windows byml-v2. Mac byml-v2. Linux byml-v2.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".QR",
    "Quaver Replay",
    "4.0",
    "A QR file is a video created by Quaver, a rhythm game available for Windows and macOS. It contains a replay of a players Quaver gameplay. QR files can be played in Quaver. Quaver is an open-source rhythm game in which players press buttons in time to music. After a player completes a Quaver level, they can watch a replay of their current or previous gameplay. Players can also export replays as QR files, to watch again later or share with others. QR files are video files that can be played only in Quaver. However, some players have managed to convert their replays to.MP4 files, using tools like Convertio.com. You can play a QR file in Quaver (Windows, Mac). To do so, open Quaver and drag your QR file to the games main menu. Windows Quaver. Mac QuaverFree.",
    "Windows Quaver. Mac Quaver.",
    "?"],
