    ["game_files",
    ".MCLEVEL",
    "Minecraft Map File",
    "3.9",
    "Game level created by Minecraft, a single player or multiplayer first-person perspective game; contains information for a game map, including the location of 3D building blocks, spawn points, sky colors, and map size; stored in a proprietary tagged binary format called the Named Binary Tag format, or NBT for short. Minecraft is a Java-based program that can be run in a Web browser. In Minecraft, users create and destroy blocks and mine resources in a 3D environment. NOTE: MCLEVEL files were used by early releases of Minecraft (called indev) and are not compatible with current versions of the software. At the time MCLEVEL files were used, only registered users of Minecraft were able to save MCLEVEL files. Windows Mojang Minecraft. Mac Mojang Minecraft. Linux Mojang Minecraft.",
    "Windows Mojang Minecraft. Mac Mojang Minecraft. Linux Mojang Minecraft.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".BAR",
    "Age of Empires 3 Data File",
    "3.9",
    "Data file used by Age of Empires III, a strategy game where players control units and build empires; may include game data, such as textures, music, fonts, text, and other game settings; serves as a file container that holds game data. BAR files are loaded by Age of Empires III on-demand during gameplay. Windows Microsoft Age of Empires 3. Dragon UnPACKer. AoE3Ed. File used by Age of Mythology, a Microsoft real-time strategy (RTS) game where player control units in a mythological era; serves as a file container which includes game data, such as textures, music, maps, models, and other game settings. BAR files are commonly used to create custom modifications for game content. Windows Microsoft Age of Mythology. Dragon UnPACKer. AOM EditFree. A BAR file is a Binary Application Resource used by Brew Mobile Platform (MP) applications. It specifies the assets available to an executing module. BAR files are compiled from.CAR files and loaded when the application is run. Windows Qualcomm Brew MP SDK Mac Qualcomm Brew MP SDK Linux Qualcomm Brew MP SDK. A BAR file contains data used to load customized XnViewMP toolbars. It defines the buttons and separators that each of XnViewMPs toolbars (Browser and View) contains. XnViewMP users can create and save custom toolbars using the programs Customize toolbar.. feature. BAR file open in XnViewMP XnViewMP is an image viewer that allows users to open, edit, and convert images saved in over 500 different formats. The program contains both a file browser and an image viewer, each of which uses its own toolbar. Users can customize either of XnViewMPs toolbars by right-clicking in the toolbar and selecting Customize toolbar.. This allows the user to add additional toolbar buttons, remove buttons, move buttons within a toolbar, add separators, add custom toolbar commands, and more. If a user wants to save their custom toolbar settings, so they can load their custom toolbars in another instance of XnViewMP, they can do so by selecting the Save.. button that appears in the toolbar Settings window. This saves the users toolbar settings as a BAR file. The BAR file is a plain text file broken into two sections, one for XnViewMPs Browser toolbar and another for its View toolbar. Each section contains a list of the buttons and separators used in its associated toolbar. To use a BAR file as an XnViewMP custom toolbar: Open XnViewMP. Right-click in the programs toolbar. Select Customize toolbar.. Select Load.. Navigate to and open your BAR file. Select OK. If necessary, you can also open a BAR file in Microsoft Notepad (Windows), Apple TextEdit (Mac), or any other text editor, to view the plain text data the file contains. Windows XnViewMP. Any text editor Mac XnViewMP. Any text editor Linux XnViewMP. Any text editor.",
    "Windows Microsoft Age of Empires 3. Dragon UnPACKer. AoE3Ed. Windows Microsoft Age of Mythology. Dragon UnPACKer. AOM Edit. Windows Qualcomm Brew MP SDK. Mac Qualcomm Brew MP SDK. Linux Qualcomm Brew MP SDK. Windows XnViewMP. Any text editor. Mac XnViewMP. Any text editor. Linux XnViewMP. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["game_files",
    ".ESP",
    "Fallout 3 Plugin File",
    "3.9",
    "An ESP file is a plug-in used by The Elder Scrolls IV: Oblivion, The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim, or Fallout 4, which are video games developed by Bethesda Softworks. ESP files are similar to.ESM files, except they always contain add-on game content, such as new graphics, weapons, or world content. Players often upload and distribute ESP files over the Internet. Bethesda encourages Oblivion, Skyrim, and Fallout 4 players to mod the games if they so desire. To that end, the games each feature the ability to load additional content stored in ESP files. Players can also modify the games using the following file types: Elder Scrolls Master File (.esm file) - The games primary data files. ESP files can modify, add to, or reference these files. Players also sometimes modify ESM files directly. Elder Scrolls Light Master File (.esl file) - These game data files use an optimized, lighter version of the ESM format, which was introduced with the Fallout 4 patch 1.10 in August 2017. As the games load, they first load the data contained in their ESM master files. Then, they apply any game modification data youve added in the form of ESP files. This allows the ESP files to override the games default data and behavior. You can use ESP files to modify various Bethesda Softworks games, including Fallout 4, The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim, Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim Special Edition (SE), and The Elder Scrolls IV: Oblivion. You can also view ESP files contents with Bethesda Softworks Creation Kit (Windows) and The Elder Scrolls Construction Set (Windows). Windows Bethesda Softworks The Elder Scrolls 5: Skyrim. Bethesda Softworks The Elder Scrolls 5: Skyrim SE. Bethesda Softworks The Elder Scrolls 4: Oblivion. Bethesda Softworks Fallout 4. The Elder Scrolls Construction Set. Bethesda Softworks Creation KitFree. Plugin file used by the Fallout 3 and Fallout: New Vegas futuristic post-nuclear war role-playing games; contains a plugin that modifies game content; can include game objects, dialogue, gameplay settings, AI settings, world properties, and other content. ESP files can be used for modifying existing game data or for adding new game data. They can be edited using G.E.C.K. (the Garden of Eden Creation Kit), which is made available by Bethesda Softworks, the developers of the Fallout series. ESP plugin files are complementary to.ESM master files and will not load without a corresponding ESM file. Additionally, the settings in ESP files override those in ESM files, since the ESP game data is always loaded after the ESM data. Therefore, you can use ESP files to override the default game properties with custom properties. Windows Bethesda Softworks Fallout 3. Bethesda Softworks Fallout: New Vegas. Bethesda Softworks GECKFree. Contains data generated by Advanced Chemistry Development analysis software. Windows ACD/SpecManager or ACD/Structure Elucidator.",
    "Windows Bethesda Softworks The Elder Scrolls 5: Skyrim. Bethesda Softworks The Elder Scrolls 5: Skyrim SE. Bethesda Softworks The Elder Scrolls 4: Oblivion. Bethesda Softworks Fallout 4. The Elder Scrolls Construction Set. Bethesda Softworks Creation Kit. Windows Bethesda Softworks Fallout 3. Bethesda Softworks Fallout: New Vegas. Bethesda Softworks GECK. Windows ACD/SpecManager or ACD/Structure Elucidator.",
    "?"],
